      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "87c70b50-d6f5-5cc7-b6b6-c36173a9b695",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jefferson Davis, Alexander H. Stephens, Franklin Pierce\n- Roles: President of the Confederate States, Vice President of the Confederate States, United States Secretary of War\n- Places: White House of the Confederacy\n- Organizations: Confederate States of America, United States government\n- Concepts: Provisional Confederate States Constitution, Confederate presidential election of 1861\n\nFacts:\n- In 1818, the White House of the Confederacy was built.\n- From 1853 to 1857, Jefferson Davis served as the 23rd United States Secretary of War under U.S. President Franklin Pierce.\n- In 1861, under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution, Jefferson Davis was elected President and Alexander H. Stephens was elected Vice President of the Confederate States of America.\n- Jefferson Davis and Alexander H. Stephens were elected to six-year terms as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America from February 22, 1862, to February 22, 1868.\n- From August 1861 until April 1865, the White House of the Confederacy was the main executive residence of Jefferson Davis, the only President of the Confederate States of America.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "18eb0eba-7e9c-55fe-82a6-c168e4855d29",
        "text": "Page: Confederate States presidential election, 1861. Sentence: Jefferson Davis, who had been elected President and Alexander H. Stephens, who had been elected Vice President, under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution, were elected to six-year terms (February 22, 1862 – February 22, 1868) as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America.\nPage: Jefferson Davis. Sentence: He was the 23rd United States Secretary of War, serving under U.S. President Franklin Pierce from 1853 to 1857.\nPage: White House of the Confederacy. Sentence: Built in 1818, it was the main executive residence of the sole President of the Confederate States of America, Jefferson Davis, from August 1861 until April 1865.",
